<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.13.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01477</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.13.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ee7de441-038b-479d-8fdc-c0aacc860fa8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have
    learned in this lesson.</emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is a graph of \(p\), an insect population, \(w\) weeks after it was first measured. The population grows
    exponentially. [Teacher note: Graph is drawn to scale, but students may have trouble since at \(t=0\), \(p=300\),
    and that is not marked on the graph. Students are forced to find the growth factor and work backward.]<figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/be8f4718ec44f2071d0a890cab99468e1bc5a1c2"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>
        What is the weekly factor of growth for the insect population? </item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 3
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      What was the population when it was first measured? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>300
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      Write an equation relating \(p\) and \(w\)<emphasis>.</emphasis>
      <list>
        <item>
          \(p=300+3^w\)
        </item>
        <item>
          \(p=300 \cdot 3^w\)
        </item>
        <item>
          \(p= \frac{300}{3^w}\)
        </item>
        <item>
          \(p=-300 \cdot 3^w\)

        </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(p=300 \cdot 3^w\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is a graph of the function \(f\) defined by \(f(x)=a \cdot b^x\).<figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8557128fefac88efa9d2e444483c4358ec43784e"/></media></figure><emphasis>
      Select three</emphasis> possible values of \(b\).
  </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(0\)</item>
  <item> \(\frac{1}{10}\)</item>
  <item>\(\frac12\)</item>
  <item> \(\frac{9}{10}\)</item>
  <item> \(1\)</item>
  <item> \(1.3\)</item>
  <item> \(\frac{18}{5}\)
  </item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>b, c, and d</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>The function \(f\) is given by \(f(x)=50\cdot (\frac{1}{2})^x\), and the function \(g\) is given by \(g(x)=50
    \cdot (\frac{1}{3})^x\).<figure><media alt="Graph of two decreasing exponential functions, xy-plane, origin O."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6414b8e8e85d787caa65ad053cd0afa0f6c657ac"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Graph 1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>The function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=50 \cdot 3^x\). The function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=a \cdot b^x\).<figure><media alt="Graph of two increasing exponential functions, labeled f and g, xy-plane, origin O."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3e66cf332bc430de8285567ea13166eae76e7e1d"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>
        In \(g(x)\), \(a\)is greater than/less than 50. </item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> greater than
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      In \(g(x)\), \(b\) is greater than/less than 3.
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> less than
</para></section>
</content>
